Feature: Program-scoped typed neighborhood calculation
  Neighbor is calculated for a declared query from distinct structural, geometric,
  occurrence, semantic, and epistemic channels. No single stored score replaces them.

  Scenario: Exact structural neighborhood requires no testimony
    Given a bit-perfect physicality trajectory with nested containers, runs, tiers, and repeated constituents
    And no source has attested any semantic relation among those constituents
    When Laplace calculates the structural neighborhood in bulk
    Then container, constituent, ancestor, descendant, sibling, ordinal, preceding, following, tier, run, and recurrence results are exact
    And each result closes to the trajectory bytes, physicality recipe, geometry epoch, and calculation receipt
    And changing source trust or consensus cannot change those structural results

  Scenario: Metric families remain observably distinct
    Given crafted points, curves, sets, trajectories, and manifold samples whose angular, Fréchet, Hausdorff, Karcher-derived, Hilbert, and structural orderings disagree
    When each neighborhood family is calculated
    Then each family returns the independently calculated expected ordering in its declared units
    And replacing them with one scalar affinity makes at least one deliberate negative control fail

  Scenario: Karcher calculations cannot replace composite physicality centroid
    Given an asymmetric composition whose arithmetic centroid differs from its Karcher mean
    When Laplace calculates composite physicality and a Karcher-derived neighborhood
    Then physicality retains the four-dimensional arithmetic centroid of its actual constituent points
    And the Karcher result remains a separately typed query calculation with its own receipt
    And projecting the composite back to S3 fails the physicality contract

  Scenario: Glicko standing retains the complete evidence boundary
    Given candidate relations differ by source trust, source-type trust, relation type, independent roots, copied ancestry, contradiction, context, and consensus epoch
    When a query requests epistemic neighborhood under a pinned epoch
    Then Glicko-2 standing is calculated from only eligible evidence and declared matchups
    And one root with one thousand descendants still contributes one independent root
    And changing the epoch or eligible context changes only the epistemic query result
    And the receipt identifies the ratings, deviations, volatilities, evidence roots, trust dimensions, relation types, and epoch

  Scenario: Relation importance is conditioned by the objective
    Given the same sentence contains a noun, function words, ordinals, and punctuation
    When one query resolves referential subject and another reproduces exact surface form
    Then their declared importance functions select different decisive constituents
    And neither query publishes a permanent claim that one constituent class is universally more important
    And replacing both functions with one timeless part-of-speech weight fails the expected query behavior

  Scenario: Calculated ranking is not canonical authority
    Given a query calculates a scalar rank from selected neighborhood components
    When the rank artifact is deleted and regenerated from the same pinned inputs
    Then the regenerated ordering and receipt agree under the numeric contract
    And entity, physicality, occurrence, testimony, consensus, and inference state remain unchanged
    And writing the query rank back as a universal relation fails layer separation

  Scenario: Every orchestration route executes one neighborhood program
    Given one typed bulk neighborhood program and one pinned substrate boundary
    When native, PostgreSQL SPI, SQL, and C# orchestration routes execute it
    Then component values, ordering, ties, selected set, and receipts agree
    And no route substitutes a private metric or importance implementation

  Scenario: A cognition query compiles an indexed typed state search
    Given a goal requires containment, ordinal, semantic, source-standing, and geometric transitions
    When Laplace compiles its search program
    Then the initial states, terminal predicate, enabled transition families, hard constraints, accumulated costs, remaining-cost estimate, evidence epoch, and tie rules are explicit
    And native and PostgreSQL indexes generate each eligible frontier family in bounded bulk batches
    And no scalar graph or permanent vector space replaces the typed state transitions

  Scenario: An A-star optimality claim requires a valid heuristic contract
    Given a small independently solved typed state space with nonnegative query costs
    When A-star executes with an admissible consistent heuristic
    Then the selected terminal path matches the independent optimum
    And the receipt proves canonical state identity, heuristic bounds, duplicate handling, tie rules, and completion
    But an overestimating heuristic without declared bounded-search semantics fails the optimality claim
    And inconsistent heuristics without explicit reopen behavior fail the search contract

  Scenario: Metric nearest retrieval is only a frontier generator
    Given the nearest entities under one geometric metric do not satisfy the query evidence and containment constraints
    When that metric top-k result seeds the search frontier
    Then the cognition program expands or rejects candidates using its other typed constraints
    And it returns the supported goal state rather than the metric-nearest invalid entity
    And substituting top-k distance for the complete cognition program fails expected behavior

  Scenario: Search path retains typed provenance
    Given two paths reach the same entity through different physicality, testimony, and consensus transitions
    When the query selects one path under a pinned goal and epoch
    Then every transition retains its type, source layer, cost components, index expansion, evidence roots, and calculation receipt
    And equal endpoint identity cannot collapse the two paths before the query's canonical-state rule permits it

  Scenario: Search replay is deterministic across orchestration routes
    Given one pinned substrate boundary, search program, machine profile, and index epoch
    When native, PostgreSQL SPI, SQL, and C# routes execute the program
    Then expansion order, duplicate and reopen decisions, pruned states, terminal result, and receipt agree
    And changing physical plans without changing logical semantics preserves the accepted result

  Scenario: Declared A-star priority actually uses the certified heuristic
    Given an admissible nonzero heuristic changes the order of unequal accumulated-cost frontier states
    When the search program declares A-star semantics
    Then frontier priority is the declared combination of accumulated and remaining cost
    And the accepted result agrees with an independent least-cost oracle
    But ordering accumulated cost first and consulting the heuristic only on exact ties is reported as uniform-cost search rather than A-star

  Scenario: Bounded state identity preserves a feasible shallower arrival
    Given one entity can be reached by a cheap deep state and a costlier shallow state
    And only the shallow state has enough remaining depth to reach the goal
    When bounded search deduplicates frontier states
    Then its canonical state key or dominance law preserves the feasible shallow state
    And keying only by entity identity does not suppress the accepted path

  Scenario: Requested path multiplicity is executed
    Given a query requests three distinct accepted paths with declared diversity and ordering rules
    When the search program completes
    Then it returns three paths when three qualifying paths exist
    And every path has an independent transition receipt and deterministic rank
    And ignoring the requested path count fails the result contract

  Scenario: Frontier expansion is set-oriented
    Given one search iteration contains many frontier states sharing indexed transition families
    When native PostgreSQL execution expands the frontier
    Then it issues bounded set-oriented expansion operations rather than one database call per state
    And the receipt reports frontier size, rows examined, index plans, crossings, emitted states, and elapsed time

  Scenario: Scalar edge cost cannot erase the typed transition
    Given two transitions reach the same target with the same scalar cost but differ in layer, direction, relation law, source, context, and evidence roots
    When search evaluates and returns a path
    Then the transitions remain distinct until the program's declared equivalence rule permits merging
    And target plus scalar cost alone cannot reconstruct or certify the selected path
